Non-vacuity: unlike T02 and T03, this task did NOT prove its assertions by
temporarily breaking production code — T04 does not own any production file, and
mutating one would violate its declared-file ownership. The equivalent
protection is built into the suite itself, which is why tasks.md required it:
  - the scanner guard asserts the walk sees exactly Program.cs,
    ModuleRegistration.cs, and PortalContent.cs, and that known-present patterns
    (AddTaxAiModules, MapGet, IT4LineMapProvider) still match — so a broken walk
    or an over-eager comment filter cannot make every negative assertion pass
    for free;
  - the comment-filter guard proves the filter removes documentation naming a
    forbidden concept (IDocumentExtractionIngress) while leaving code
    (AddSingleton) intact, in both directions;
  - the AC-22 assertion checks not only that no registration lives outside the
    composition root, but that the composition root contains exactly three — so
    it cannot pass by the registrations having quietly disappeared.

ENVIRONMENT — resolved during this task:
  The suite could not execute at first. Windows Smart App Control was in
  enforcement mode (HKLM:\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\CI\Policy\
  VerifiedAndReputablePolicyState = 1) and blocked the freshly built
  TaxAI.Portal.dll with 0x800711C7, skipping the entire portal suite. The same
  policy had blocked TaxCases.Tests.dll during T02. Three retries over several
  minutes did not clear it, and no workaround was attempted: clearing it means
  changing a machine security policy, which is the human's call. The human
  turned Smart App Control off (state now 0) and the suite ran green on its
  first execution afterwards. Note that turning SAC off is not reversible
  without a Windows reset.

  The related hazard first flagged in T02 is still NOT fixed and is not this
  task's to fix: `dotnet test TaxAI.sln` returns exit code 0 when an assembly is
  skipped, so a green exit code alone is not proof that every test ran. Every
  wave gate in this workflow trusts that exit code. A per-project test-count
  floor would make a skipped assembly loud instead of silent.

Merged by: not merged — awaiting human review. Agents never merge.
